The empirical relation $\frac{x}{m}=k p^{\frac{1}{n}}$, is known as the Freundlich adsorption isotherm. Taking the logarithm on both sides, $\log \frac{x}{m}=\log k=\frac{1}{n} \log p  \text{$ If a following curve is plotted }
$$
<img src="https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/neet/eGcNjp3jqx27y33Hvgxlq2o-90ANPjtkgaHMB1Ew28Y.original.fullsize.png"><br>

Freundlich gave an empirical relationship between the quantity of gas adsorbed by unit mass of solid adsorbent and concentration at a particular temperature.

\begin{aligned}
& $\log x$ / m=$\log K+\frac{1}{n}\log$C \\
& x=$\text{ mass$ of gas adsorbed, } m=$\text{ mass$ of adsorbent } \\
& C=$\text{ concentration, }$ k, n=$\text{ constants. }$
\end{aligned}
